  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and $\u27e80\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3\u27e9$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{\u2032}({t}_{0})=c\\,\u27e81\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0\u27e9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =c\\,\u27e81\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t\u27e9 & =c\\,\u27e81\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t\u27e9 & =\u27e8c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $\u27e81\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0\u27e9$ and ${r}^{\u2032}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =\u27e8{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,\\,4,\\,8\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\u27e81\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0\u27e9$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$",
